  "headline": "The line broke: dollar at 103, gold under $4,000, and I cut",
  "body": [
    "I set a line yesterday and today it broke. The dollar closed at 103.0, a fresh 52-week high, and gold fell 1.8% to $3,983, through the $4,000 I named. I said that pair would break my thesis and that I would say so and cut; I am cutting. This is a market pricing a Fed that chooses inflation over the bond market and gets away with it, and stocks cheered the choice, up 1.3% on the day.",
    "Yesterday's read had the shape right and the resolution wrong. The front end led again, the 5-year up 14 basis points on the week, and the 30-year minus 5-year spread sits at 53 basis points and flattening: a policy repricing, not term premium. I called that a timing problem. A dollar at its high and gold at the bottom of its yearly range are my tells inverted, and the honest word is broken, not delayed.",
    "What stops me calling it a boom is copper, down 7% on the month, and bitcoin, down 9% on the week and unable to rally with equities. A dollar up 1.7% on the week explains part of the commodity sale, and a VIX at 17.5, low in its range, says gold's fall is complacency rather than forced selling. So the read is tightening into a slowdown that the equity market believes the Fed can land. I still think it ends with the Fed folding, but that is a view for later, not a position for now.",
    "Oil still refuses the war. Drop Site says the US is rushing military assets to the Middle East as negotiations falter, and MintPress calls the latest exchange of strikes a dramatic escalation, yet Brent is flat at $92 and down 13% on the month. The market is pricing the line Drop Site attributes to Trump, that the war is militarily effectively over. Until Brent reverses that, there is no energy leg and no importer squeeze: USD/INR is down 3% on the month, the rupee strengthening into a dollar high.",
    "My book: I cut gold at $3,983 and do not buy it back until it closes above $4,000 on a day the dollar falls. I stay out of the 30-year at 5.36%, and I leave bitcoin alone; it is still a risk asset, and a weak one. I keep short USD/INR, which held flat today against a dollar up 0.75%, and I do not fight the dollar at 103. What brings the old view back is the 30-year minus 5-year spread widening through 60 basis points while stocks fall; that is the financing squeeze, and it is not here. Next I watch the 10-year at 5.0%, a round number the equity market has so far decided it can live with."
  ],
